#Day9
Create a kubernetes deployment named httpd to deploy the application httpd using the image httpd:latest (ensure to specify the tag).

1. To create kubernetes deployment, it is recommended (but not mandatory) to have kubectl utility.

2. There are two option, fist with imperative command option which is using kubectl command directly and second is declarative YAML file (recommended for production).

3. Let's check imperative command option in below mentioned example
    kubectl create deployment NAME --image=image --replicas=NUMBER --port=CONTAINER_PORT

Reference: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/kubernetes-basics/deploy-app/deploy-intro/ 
    NAME: The name you want to give your deployment (e.g., nginx-deployment).
    image: The container image to use (e.g., nginx:1.15.0).
    --replicas: (Optional) The desired number of pod instances (default is 1).
    --port: (Optional) The port the container exposes

4. To create a deployment with name httpd and image httpd:latest we will use above example and change required options as per our need as shown in below command

    kubectl create deployment httpd --image=httpd:latest --replicas=1 --port=8080

5. The above command will create a deployment with name httpd and try to pull docker image httpd with tag latest. You can check deployment and pod using below commands

    kubectl get deployment OR kubectl get deploy // This will list all the deployment in default namespace.

    kubectl get pods // This will list all pods in default namespace.

6. The second option is using declarative YAML file. Below is the example of YAML file which will have all required details. 

    //YAML File content starts here

    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    metadata:
      name: httpd
    spec:
      selector:
        matchLabels:
          app: httpd
    replicas: 1 # Tells Kubernetes to run 1 instances of your app
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: httpd
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: httpd
            image: httpd:latest # Specify the container image
            ports:
            - containerPort: 8080

    //YAML file content ends here

7. We have to be cautious about the indentation of content under YAML file. Next, we will need to apply settings mentioned in above yAML file using below command.

    kubectl apply -f deployment_file.yaml

8. Once above kubectl command is executed, new deployment with name httpd will be created. You can view deployment and pod using below commands.

    kubectl get deployment OR kubectl get deploy 

    kubectl get pods
